<h2>Your task</h2>
<p>Choose one real place in the Chennai area. Explain how it was made, what is shaping it now, and what it could look like in 50 years. Finish with one recommendation.</p>
<p>You are writing for a planning meeting at the Greater Chennai Corporation. The people in the room are not scientists. They need clear language, real evidence, and an honest statement of what is uncertain.</p>

<h2>Step 1: Choose your location</h2>
<p>Your location is locked in class: {{ "R2" | sessionLabel }}.</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Location</th><th>Type</th><th>Why it is interesting</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Cooum River basin</td><td>Urban river</td><td>Heavily modified, with restoration under way. Many sources, and many of them disagree</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Adyar River and Estuary</td><td>Urban river and coast</td><td>River and coastal processes meet here</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Marina Beach</td><td>Coast</td><td>A very wide beach with a reason for its width</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Pulicat Lagoon</td><td>Coastal wetland</td><td>Less written about, so the source work is harder and more rewarding</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Mahabalipuram coast</td><td>Coast and heritage</td><td>Very old rock, 1300 years of carving, and an active shoreline</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Eastern Ghats foothills</td><td>Inland hills</td><td>Tropical weathering away from the coast</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Buckingham Canal</td><td>Human-made canal</td><td>A built channel that surface processes are slowly taking back</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Red Hills and Sholavaram Lake area</td><td>Reservoir</td><td>The city's water supply, shaped by people and by the monsoon</td></tr>
    <tr><td>AISC campus walk</td><td>Campus</td><td>Drainage, soil and exposed rock at a small scale</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>You may propose another place you know well. Bring the idea to the class where locations are locked.</p>

<p>The Location Data Cards in Google Classroom give a starting point for each of the nine locations. Treat them like any other source: they were made by your teacher, they simplify, and some details may have changed. Check a card's key sources before you cite something from it.</p>

<h2>Step 2: Gather your data</h2>
<p>Have this ready before the drafting class: {{ "R3" | sessionLabel }}.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Google Earth, including the historical imagery slider</li>
  <li>One map (topographic, geological, or drainage)</li>
  <li>Two sources. One must not be AI</li>
</ul>

<h2>Step 3: Write four short sections</h2>
<p><strong>1. How this place was made.</strong> Three time points:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Deep past: what are the rocks or sediments, and how did they get here?</li>
  <li>Historical past: when and why did people start changing this place?</li>
  <li>The last 50 years: what has changed? Use the historical imagery.</li>
</ul>
<p>Be specific. "This place is very old" is not a biography.</p>
<p><strong>2. What is shaping it now.</strong> Name two surface processes that are active at your location. For each one, point to something you can see in an image, map or photo, and give its date.</p>
<p><strong>3. Fifty years from now.</strong> Choose one scenario and follow it through your location:</p>
<ul>
  <li>current trends continue, or</li>
  <li>the monsoon becomes more extreme, with heavier rain and longer dry periods, or</li>
  <li>sea level rises by about half a meter.</li>
</ul>
<p>Say what changes first, and what that change causes next.</p>
<p><strong>4. One recommendation.</strong> What should the city monitor or protect at this location, and why? Say how confident you are.</p>

<h2>Step 4: Source page</h2>
<p>List your sources on a separate page. Write one or two lines of <a href="/foundations/opvl-framework/">OPVL</a> for each. Name the Limitation that matters most for how you used the source.</p>

