<h2>Using AI</h2>
<p>You may use AI to find sources, interpret images, check your reasoning and edit your wording. Document every use with the <a href="/student-hub/ai-documentation-template/">AI Documentation Template</a> from Unit 0. If you used AI and the documentation is missing, your work will be returned as incomplete and your score held until it is submitted.</p>
<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">Working with the Field Geologist</div>
  <p>Your AI partner for this task: <a href="/ai-partners/field-geologist/">the Field Geologist</a>.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>It cannot see your images. Describe what you see first, then ask what it means.</li>
    <li>It will ask you questions before it answers yours. That is on purpose.</li>
    <li>If it gives you a number, a date or a rock age, check it against GSI, Bhuvan or another primary source before you use it.</li>
    <li>It will not write your sections for you.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A good way to start:</p>
  <p><em>"I am working on the Adyar estuary. In the 2002 Google Earth image I see ___. In the 2025 image I see ___. Which processes could explain the difference?"</em></p>
</div>

<h2>Peer check</h2>
<p>In class, before you submit: {{ "R5" | sessionLabel }}. Swap with a partner and answer four questions about their Reading:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Can you find three time points in Section 1?</li>
  <li>Does each process in Section 2 point to something visible, with a date?</li>
  <li>Does Section 3 say what happens first and what follows?</li>
  <li>Would a city planner understand the recommendation without a science dictionary?</li>
</ol>

<h2>Before you submit</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Four sections, in order</li>
  <li>Two processes, each with visible evidence and a date</li>
  <li>One scenario followed through at least two steps</li>
  <li>One recommendation with a confidence statement</li>
  <li>Source page with OPVL</li>
  <li>AI Documentation, if AI was used</li>
  <li>600 words or fewer, or 3 minutes, or one page</li>
</ul>

<h2>Rubric</h2>
<p>Each strand is scored on the department 8-point scale and reported separately. The strands are not added together.</p>

<h3>Knowledge and Understanding (K/U)</h3>
<p><em>The student recalls facts, terms and definitions while also explaining concepts, ideas, processes, themes, and the like. The student learns and performs skills related to their studies.</em></p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>7-8 Exemplary</th><th>5-6 Proficient</th><th>3-4 Developing</th><th>1-2 Limited</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Weathering, erosion and deposition processes are correctly identified at the location and explained with their mechanisms.</td><td>Most processes are correctly identified. Mechanisms are mostly accurate, with minor errors.</td><td>Processes are named but the mechanisms are vague ("water erodes things") or misapplied.</td><td>Processes are missing, generic or misidentified.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The biography gives three time points, each with a process that fits the period.</td><td>The biography gives three time points with reasonable accuracy.</td><td>A time point is missing, or claims are unsupported.</td><td>The biography is vague or factually incorrect.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Course vocabulary is used accurately throughout, and monsoon and coastal processes are applied correctly to Chennai.</td><td>Vocabulary is accurate most of the time. Chennai context is mostly applied correctly.</td><td>Vocabulary is inconsistent. The Chennai context is generic.</td><td>Vocabulary is largely absent or misused.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3>Thinking and Transfer (T/T)</h3>
<p><em>The student plans for learning by formulating questions, generating ideas, and researching. The student thinks about learning critically by analyzing, interpreting, evaluating, and using other metacognitive skills. The student applies knowledge and skills to unfamiliar or new contexts.</em></p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>7-8 Exemplary</th><th>5-6 Proficient</th><th>3-4 Developing</th><th>1-2 Limited</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Claims rest on specific visible evidence with dates (channel changes, shoreline position, weathering features).</td><td>Most claims are supported by evidence. Some are general.</td><td>Evidence is listed but not reasoned from.</td><td>Mostly opinion or unsupported claims.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The projection follows one scenario through the location's own features, with each change causing the next.</td><td>The projection fits the scenario and the location, with one clear consequence.</td><td>The projection is vague, or would fit any location.</td><td>No projection, or one unrelated to the scenario.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>OPVL is applied to at least two sources. At least one Limitation is specific and changes how the source is used.</td><td>OPVL is applied. Limitations are present but general.</td><td>OPVL is weak or generic.</td><td>OPVL is trivial or missing.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The recommendation follows from the analysis, is realistic, and states its confidence.</td><td>The recommendation is reasonable but may overstate certainty.</td><td>The recommendation is generic ("protect this place").</td><td>No recommendation, or one unrelated to the analysis.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3>Communication (C)</h3>
<p><em>The student expresses and organizes ideas in multiple forms. The student adapts communication appropriately to different audiences and forms. The student uses conventions, terminology and vocabulary relevant to the discipline.</em></p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>7-8 Exemplary</th><th>5-6 Proficient</th><th>3-4 Developing</th><th>1-2 Limited</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>The four sections are clear and in a logical order. The writing suits a planning audience without losing accuracy.</td><td>Mainly clear and logically ordered. The voice sometimes slips into a school-report register.</td><td>Somewhat clear. The structure is attempted but hard to follow. Reads as a student paper.</td><td>Unclear or disorganized.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Technical terms are used precisely and explained briefly where a non-scientist would need it.</td><td>Terms are precise most of the time.</td><td>Terms are used with errors or without explanation.</td><td>Terms are missing or misused.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Sources are cited where they are used, and the source page is complete. Within the length limit.</td><td>Most sources are cited. Within the length limit.</td><td>Citations are incomplete, or the limit is exceeded.</td><td>Sources are missing or not cited.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

